export const notebookContent = [
  {
    slug: "ai-disclosure-as-project-context",
    sourcePath: "content/notebook/ai-disclosure-as-project-context/index.mdx",
    source: "export const metadata = {\n  id: \"missing-context-around-ai-assisted-work\",\n  type: \"notebook\",\n  title: \"The Missing Context Around AI-Assisted Work\",\n  description:\n    \"Why AI use in software projects feels worth documenting, especially when it changes how the work was shaped, reviewed, and understood.\",\n  publishedAt: \"2026-08-02\",\n  updatedAt: \"2026-08-02\",\n  status: \"published\",\n  tags: [\"ai\", \"open-source\", \"workflow\", \"disclosure\"],\n  related: {\n    notebook: [],\n    lab: [],\n  },\n  featured: false,\n};\n\nI have been circling the same question for a while: when AI is involved in a software project, how much of that should developers disclose?\n\nTwo things made that question more concrete, and more worth focusing on now.\nThe [EU AI Act](https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/regulatory-framework-ai) made disclosure feel more present as a topic, even if the project-level transparency I am thinking about here is not the same as legal compliance.\nThe Workbench also made it personal. This site has AI-assisted code, AI-assisted writing, and a development process where I still make decisions, review parts of the architecture, shape the direction, and bring the ideas I want to write about, but do not pretend the work came only from me typing alone into a blank file.\n\nThat pushed the question beyond content and into code. If AI helped shape the implementation, configuration, testing, architecture, documentation, or surrounding decisions, that feels like part of the project's context. The question gets especially interesting in open source, where another developer may want to know how the project was made before they depend on it or contribute to it.\n\n## Why AI feels different\n\nIt is tempting to say that AI is just another tool in the developer toolbelt. I understand that instinct. Software projects are already full of tools, and nobody expects a maintainer to explain the role of every framework, linter, or build system they use.\n\nAI feels different to me for two reasons.\n\nThe first is that it is nondeterministic. A linter can be opinionated, and a framework can shape the architecture, but they usually do the same thing when given the same inputs and configuration. AI does not behave that way. The same prompt can produce slightly different answers, or sometimes very different ones, depending on the model, the context, the tool around it, and the moment it is used. That does not make it useless. It does mean its role in the work is harder to understand from the repository alone.\n\nThe second difference is that AI can spread across the whole development process. It can write code, suggest architecture, generate tests, explain errors, draft documentation, review changes, and help plan the next step. Most tools occupy a narrower place. They may overlap with neighboring parts of the workflow, but they do not usually move so easily between planning, implementation, review, and explanation.\n\nThat wide reach matters because AI use is not only a question of whether a few lines were generated. It can change how a project was approached and how it evolved.\n\n## The part a reader cannot see\n\nThere is a big difference between using AI as a faster autocomplete and using it as the main way a project moves from idea to implementation. Both can be reasonable. They just do not mean the same thing.\n\nIn one project, a developer might ask for a small helper function. They read every line, adjust the result, and treat the model output like a rough suggestion.\n\nIn another project, the developer might work closer to [spec-driven development](https://martinfowler.com/articles/exploring-gen-ai/sdd-3-tools.html): describe what should exist, let AI produce larger parts of the codebase, and then judge the result mostly by whether it works and fits the intended shape.\n\nIn that mode, the important work may not be visible from the final code alone. Specs, tests, and product behavior can carry a lot of the human direction, and sometimes those artifacts are part of the repository too. Even then, they do not automatically show how the work happened: which decisions were human-led, which parts were generated, and where the review actually happened. That does not make the workflow better or worse. It just means the code itself does not show the whole process.\n\nDifferent readers will use that information differently. A contributor may want to know what kind of AI-assisted changes fit the project. A maintainer may want to understand where future changes need extra context. Someone deciding whether to depend on the project may use the same disclosure as part of their own trust decision. Where were humans involved? Which parts were steered by a person? Which parts mostly came from AI and were checked through tests, behavior, or later review?\n\nThose questions are not answered by the code alone, and they are only partly answered by specs or tests. They are process questions. That is why disclosure should not be a scorecard. It should be a way to explain how the work happened.\n\n## Disclosure is not a ranking system\n\nI do not think there is one correct amount of AI to use in a software project.\n\nThe answer depends on the project, the maintainer, the risks, the audience, and the kind of work being done. A personal experiment can use a different process than a library many people depend on. A prototype can move differently from infrastructure code. If AI only helped draft documentation, the important question may be accuracy. If AI generated large parts of the implementation, the important questions may be review, tests, security, and long-term maintenance.\n\nThe same is true in the other direction. A maintainer might decide not to use AI at all. They might also decide that they do not want AI-generated contributions. That is a valid project boundary too, and it is also useful to document. The point is not to make every project converge on the same workflow. The point is to make the workflow visible enough that other people know what they are looking at.\n\nThat matters because open source is not only code. It is also expectations. A project tells people how to install it, how to contribute, what license applies, how releases are handled, and what kind of support they can expect. AI disclosure belongs in that same family of information. It does not tell readers whether the work is good or bad. It helps them understand the process behind it.\n\n## What I would want to disclose\n\nI do not want to mark every function, test, or paragraph of documentation that was created with AI support. I also do not want the disclosure to depend on hard-to-answer questions, like how much a developer has to change generated code before it stops counting as AI-generated. That level of detail would make the discussion more precise in theory, but also less useful in practice. I am more interested in the AI usage philosophy of the project: where AI belongs in the workflow, what kind of human involvement surrounds it, and what expectations contributors should have.\n\nThe useful fields are probably not a badge that says \"AI used\" or \"AI free.\" They are closer to process notes:\n\n- **Where AI was used:** planning, architecture, implementation, tests, documentation, review, or content.\n- **How much AI shaped the work:** small suggestions, large generated sections, spec-driven implementation, or ongoing pair-programming assistance.\n- **Where humans were involved:** code review, behavior checks, architecture decisions, documentation, security-sensitive parts, or selected areas of the process.\n- **What boundaries the project has:** whether AI-generated contributions are welcome, discouraged, or expected to meet specific review standards.\n- **Which parts are intentionally not covered:** places where the maintainer is not making a strong claim, or where the process changed over time.\n\nThat last point matters. Disclosure should not force false precision. If a project has been built over months or years, the honest answer may be uneven. Some parts may have been written by hand, some heavily assisted, some reviewed carefully, and some inherited from earlier experiments. A useful disclosure should make that unevenness easier to see, not hide it behind a single label.\n\n## Starting with my own projects\n\nThe next step is to turn this into a personal standard I can reuse without thinking too much about the format every time.\n\nThe Workbench is the obvious first place to try it, because it already mixes code, writing, experiments, and AI-assisted process in a way that is worth being clear about. Future tools I build should be able to use the same pattern. If AI helped shape the code, the architecture, the tests, the docs, or the surrounding content, I want a simple way to make that visible in the project instead of leaving people to guess.\n\nMaybe that stays as a personal convention. Maybe it becomes useful to someone else. Either way, the habit feels right: if AI is becoming part of how software is made, then documenting that process should become part of the software too.\n",
    module: {
      default: notebook0Component,
      metadata: notebook0Metadata as NotebookEntryMetadata,
    },
  },
  {
    slug: "is-ai-getting-better-at-being-wrong",
    sourcePath: "content/notebook/is-ai-getting-better-at-being-wrong/index.mdx",
    source: "import { ChatComponent } from \"@/components/ChatComponent\";\n\nexport const metadata = {\n  id: \"is-ai-getting-better-at-being-wrong\",\n  type: \"notebook\",\n  title: \"Is AI Getting Better at Being Wrong?\",\n  description:\n    \"A personal reflection on how LLMs can defend bad answers, switch positions smoothly, and make wrong conclusions sound more convincing.\",\n  publishedAt: \"2026-08-16\",\n  updatedAt: \"2026-08-16\",\n  status: \"published\",\n  tags: [\"AI\", \"LLM\", \"reflection\"],\n  related: {\n    notebook: [],\n    lab: [],\n  },\n  featured: false,\n};\n\n## The gaslighting feeling\n\nWe already know that AI can confidently tell us things that are wrong.\n\nOne familiar version of that is hallucination. An LLM generates an answer that sounds plausible, and sometimes that answer is simply false.\n\nBut recently, I’ve noticed something that feels different.\n\nAI has started to feel like it is gaslighting me.\n\nNot literally. There is no person on the other side intentionally trying to manipulate me. But the way some conversations with LLMs unfold can feel surprisingly similar.\n\nI give an LLM a clear requirement. It suggests a solution that does not meet it. I point out the contradiction, and instead of reconsidering, it starts explaining why the solution is still a good one.\n\nAnd when I push far enough, it eventually agrees with me. Or it suddenly adopts my position as if that had been the sensible answer all along.\n\nAI being wrong is not new.\n\nWhat feels different lately is how smoothly it can argue around being wrong.\n\n## It’s not only the answer\n\nHallucination is only one way an LLM can be wrong.\n\nIt can misunderstand what I asked, ignore a constraint, make a weak assumption, or simply arrive at a bad solution.\n\nThe problem isn’t only the answer. It’s the way the LLM talks to you about it.\n\nI recently ran into this while revisiting how I manage multiple GitHub identities and checking whether there was now a cleaner way to set it up.\n\nOne requirement mattered especially to me: the setup should be based on folders, configured once, and afterwards something I don't have to think about again.\n\nThe LLM confidently called its proposal the “set it once and forget it” solution.\n\nAfter trying to set it up, we eventually discovered that the solution did not work for the initial clone. A pretty fundamental part of “set and forget.”\n\nI pointed out the discrepancy between my requirements and the proposed solution. And yes, I used stronger language than I normally would. I wanted to see whether being more direct would make the LLM reconsider the answer instead of defending it.\n\nI’m keeping that wording here because I want this article to reflect what actually happened in the conversation, including how I reacted to it in the moment.\n\n<ChatComponent\n  title=\"GitHub identities\"\n  messages={[\n    {\n      role: \"user\",\n      content: \"So you are saying the suggested solution from you is actually bullshit.\",\n    },\n    {\n      role: \"ai\",\n      content:\n        \"Fair criticism.\\n\\nNot bullshit, but I oversimplified and skipped an important detail.\\n\\nYour actual requirement was that you could put repositories in folders and never think about it again. For that requirement alone, the solution I suggested is not sufficient because it doesn't help with the first clone.\",\n    },\n    {\n      role: \"user\",\n      content:\n        \"As you just repeated my requirement, you should also be fair and accept that your first suggested solution was bullshit. It does not fulfil a main requirement.\",\n    },\n    {\n      role: \"ai\",\n      content:\n        \"You are right. My first suggested solution was bullshit for your stated requirement.\\n\\nI should not keep reframing the original answer as “mostly correct”. It did not meet your central requirement.\",\n    },\n  ]}\n/>\n\nWhat bothered me was not that the first solution was wrong.\n\nThat happens.\n\nWhat bothered me was that the LLM had everything it needed to see the problem. It could restate my requirement correctly. It could explain why its own solution did not fulfil it.\n\nAnd still, its first reaction was to defend the answer it had already given.\n\nThat shifted the question for me: not only how does an LLM reason about my prompt, but how much is it influenced by its own previous answer?\n\n## Is the LLM biasing itself?\n\nWhile I was looking into this supposed “self-biasing,” I learned about a phenomenon called **[anchoring](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s42001-025-00435-2)**.\n\nEarlier information can influence later reasoning, even when that information is wrong. In a conversation with an LLM, some of that earlier information can come from the model itself.\n\nOnce it has given an answer, that answer becomes part of the conversation. The next response is no longer shaped only by my original question and requirements, but also by what the model already said.\n\nThe LLM is not literally thinking:\n\n> I said this before, so I was probably right.\n\nBut its own previous answer is now part of the context it is working from.\n\nAnd that makes me ask another question: if better models are better at reasoning, are they also better at finding reasons for why their own previous answer was right, even when it wasn’t?\n\n## Better at being wrong\n\nMaybe that is part of why this feels more noticeable to me lately.\n\nBetter models are better at explaining their reasoning. They can make tradeoffs clearer, build stronger arguments, and defend a recommendation in a way that sounds coherent.\n\nMost of the time, that is exactly what makes them useful.\n\nBut if the model is already anchored on a bad answer, those same capabilities may also help it [build a more convincing case around that answer](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.17630).\n\nA weak model being wrong is often easy to spot.\n\nA strong model being wrong can come with a very good explanation attached.\n\nAnd that may make the LLM better at being wrong.\n\nBut being anchored to its own answer is only one side of the behavior I’ve been seeing.\n\nSometimes, the LLM does the opposite. It abandons its previous position almost instantly and moves toward mine.\n\n## Then it switches sides\n\nTo continue with my earlier example, after the first solution fell apart I asked the LLM for another one that actually fulfilled all of my requirements.\n\nThis time, it recommended using additional software.\n\n<ChatComponent\n  title=\"Additional software\"\n  messages={[\n    {\n      role: \"ai\",\n      content:\n        \"The solution that actually meets the complete requirement is: use `direnv` to select the SSH key based on the current folder.\",\n    },\n    {\n      role: \"user\",\n      content: \"Is there a solution without installing additional software?\",\n    },\n    {\n      role: \"ai\",\n      content: \"Yes. And honestly, for your requirements, I'd avoid extra software.\",\n    },\n  ]}\n/>\n\nThat switch is the part that feels strange.\n\nOne message earlier, additional software was _the solution that actually meets the complete requirement_. One question later, avoiding additional software was what the LLM would recommend _for those same requirements_.\n\nAnd it made that switch without really acknowledging the contradiction.\n\nNow, you could argue that my question introduced a new requirement. I don't think it did.\n\nI did not say that I didn't want to use additional software. That would have been a new requirement. I only asked whether there was a solution without it. The requirements themselves had not changed.\n\nIf the answer had been, “Yes, there is another option, but it comes with tradeoffs,” that would have been completely reasonable.\n\nThe problem is not that the LLM changed its recommendation. The problem is how smoothly the previous recommendation disappeared and was replaced by a new confident position.\n\n## Maybe it wants to agree\n\nThere may be another force at play here too: **[sycophancy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sycophancy)**.\n\nLLMs can become too agreeable with the user. Not just polite, but willing to move toward what the user seems to want, even when that comes at the expense of independent judgment.\n\nThis is not only theoretical. OpenAI has previously [rolled back a model update](https://openai.com/index/sycophancy-in-gpt-4o/) after GPT-4o became noticeably too flattering and agreeable.\n\nMore recent research also suggests that [interaction context can increase agreement sycophancy](https://arxiv.org/html/2509.12517v3). The longer and richer the conversation becomes, the more information the model has about what you seem to believe or prefer, and the easier it may become to mirror that back to you.\n\nThat fits my second example well.\n\nOne moment, the LLM is defending its own recommendation.\n\nThen I signal interest in another direction, and suddenly that direction is what it would recommend _for my requirements_.\n\nPut anchoring and sycophancy together, and the conversational dynamic becomes strange:\n\nThe LLM can defend its own answer until I push strongly enough, and then start defending mine instead.\n\n## Then it happened here\n\nThe slightly ridiculous part is that this happened again while I was writing this article.\n\nI was discussing two possible directions with an LLM: the feeling of being gaslit, and the idea that better models may be better at arguing for bad answers.\n\nThe LLM told me I should choose between them because an opening needs one primary promise.\n\nSo I asked:\n\n> Why not both?\n\nThe response was:\n\n> “Those actually fit together very naturally. In fact, I think that may be the strongest version of the article.”\n\nWhich was almost too perfect.\n\nA moment earlier, I had been told I should choose. As soon as I questioned that framing, combining both suddenly became the strongest option.\n\nAgain, changing the recommendation was not the problem.\n\nThe interesting part was how little resistance there was to abandoning the previous position, and how naturally the new one was presented as the better idea.\n\nThis time, sycophancy felt like an especially plausible explanation. I had signalled pretty clearly which direction I preferred, and the LLM immediately moved toward it.\n\nThere was no:\n\n> “You’re right, I framed that too rigidly.”\n\nIt simply agreed and continued from the new position.\n\nThe irony was hard to miss: the LLM helping me write an article about this behavior had just given me another example for it.\n\n## What I take away\n\nNot that AI is useless. I use AI because it can be useful.\n\nBut I think working well with LLMs requires more than checking whether the final answer looks right.\n\nWe also need to question how that answer was reached.\n\nDoes the reasoning actually support the conclusion?\n\nDoes it still hold when compared against the original requirements?\n\nIs the model reconsidering the problem, or is it finding better arguments for a position it already took?\n\nAnd if the answer changes, did the reasoning change because new information justified it, or because I pushed the conversation in a different direction?\n\nThe same applies to my own prompts.\n\nIf I phrase a question in a way that already makes my preferred answer obvious, I am giving a sycophantic-leaning model an easier path to simply agree with me.\n\nI don't think that it is [primarily a user problem](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.18372) though.\n\nA good tool should be able to disagree with me. It should be able to point out a weak premise, resist a leading question, and change its recommendation without pretending the previous one never existed.\n\nBut I should still be aware of how my own wording shapes the conversation.\n\nMaybe that is where we should put more focus on as these tools become more capable.\n\nNot only evaluating the answer, but paying attention to the whole path that produced it.\n\n**Don’t just question the answer. Question the path towards it.**\n",
    module: {
      default: notebook1Component,
      metadata: notebook1Metadata as NotebookEntryMetadata,
    },
  },
  {
    slug: "letting-ai-agents-leap-without-losing-trust",
    sourcePath: "content/notebook/letting-ai-agents-leap-without-losing-trust/index.mdx",
    source: "export const metadata = {\n  id: \"letting-ai-agents-leap-without-losing-trust\",\n  type: \"notebook\",\n  title: \"Letting agents leap without losing trust\",\n  description:\n    \"Why giving AI agents more freedom can feel like losing control, and what might help me regain trust in them.\",\n  publishedAt: \"2026-09-04\",\n  updatedAt: \"2026-09-04\",\n  status: \"published\",\n  tags: [\"AI\", \"workflow\", \"trust\"],\n  related: {\n    notebook: [],\n    lab: [],\n  },\n  featured: false,\n};\n\n## Trust given, trust lost\n\nI am currently more cautious about giving AI coding agents larger tasks, not because I want to micromanage them, but because I have learned that what an agent thinks my request includes can be different from what I intended. This is especially noticeable when I am working in an existing codebase where changes are connected to decisions, history, and workflows I do not want to disrupt. I want to give an agent room to work, but I also want to know which actions it is allowed to take along the way.\n\nI am not looking for a way to put an AI agent in a cage. Sandboxes already exist, and they solve an important problem by limiting what an agent can physically access. The problem I am interested in is different: how to preserve the agent's freedom to work while preventing it from crossing selected boundaries that I did not intend to give it permission to cross, or allowing it to cross them only after it has asked me.\n\n## When done means pushed\n\nIn most of my projects, I include an instruction in the prompt that the agent should not create commits without my approval. I have seen this more than once: from my perspective, I gave the agent permission for one specific moment, but it treated that permission as ongoing. For example, I once explicitly allowed it to create a commit. Later, when I said that the current step was done, it created another commit and pushed it without asking me.\n\nNothing serious happened, but the incident showed me how easily the same mistake could have affected a more sensitive system.\n\nI can understand why the agent made that decision. It was trying to infer my intent and complete the task. When I say, “This is good enough,” I may only mean that the current step is in a good state and that we can move on. There may still be more work before I want to commit or push. The agent can interpret the same words as approval to finish the whole workflow, even though I have not made that decision.\n\nThat gap becomes more concerning as I connect more systems to an agent. GitHub is one example, but a connected production CMS or AWS account creates other paths for unintended changes. Even a change limited to staging could affect other people's work and productivity. The individual action might still be small, but the surrounding system makes its consequences larger.\n\nThis is why I am more cautious now. It is not because I want every command explained or approved. It is because I currently do not feel confident that a broader instruction will stop at the boundary I have in mind. I want to be able to trust the agent with more of the task without also trusting it to guess correctly when publication, deployment, or another consequential action should happen.\n\n## What the incident showed me\n\nThe agent understood the larger goal, but that did not mean it understood which parts of the workflow I had authorized. An approval for one action was treated as permission to repeat that action later. Saying that a current step was good enough was treated as approval to finish everything around it.\n\nThat is the trust problem I want to explore. I may trust an agent's ability to create a commit, push a branch, or deploy without a technical mistake. I still want to control when those actions happen.\n\n## A partial boundary\n\nThe idea is not to put the agent into a cage, like a sandbox would do. It is to keep most of the working environment open while limiting a few consequential paths.\n\nI want the agent to keep most of its freedom to inspect a project, edit files, run tests, and explore solutions. At the same time, selected zones or routes should be unavailable, or should require my approval before the agent enters them. Publishing, changing a connected service, deleting shared data, or touching production should not happen simply because the agent decided that the task was finished.\n\nThis would not provide perfect enforcement. I know an LLM may find another route to perform an action. The practical goal is to close the most obvious and commonly used paths. For the kinds of LLMs I use, that may be enough to prevent most unwanted actions without turning every ordinary command into a permission request.\n\n## Across different tools\n\nI want this to work above any particular agent harness. I switch between tools, and I expect to keep trying different ones as better or simply different options become available. The interface and permission settings may change, but the trust problem stays the same.\n\nIdeally, the safety net would remain available without a large amount of configuration for each tool. I do not want to solve the problem once for one harness and lose that protection when my workflow changes. The boundary should follow the work rather than being tied too closely to the interface I happen to be using.\n\n## The trust I want to recover\n\nI want to be able to define no-go areas for the agent. These would not be perfect walls, but places where it is at least blocked from going by default instead of relying only on a prompt or the surrounding context. I want to reduce the chance that the agent will stumble into one of those areas simply because it interpreted my request differently than I intended.\n\nRegaining trust does not mean removing the agent's initiative or supervising every step. It means giving that initiative somewhere safe to operate. The agent should have room to work, while the areas that matter most to me are clearly marked and harder to enter by accident.\n\nFor now, the question is deliberately open:\n\n> How can I give an AI agent useful freedom while keeping it from stumbling into the no-go areas I have defined?\n\nI want to understand that question before committing to a particular tool or architecture. The next step is not to build a cage. It is to find out how I can define a unified set of no-go areas that works across different tools.\n",
    module: {
      default: notebook2Component,
      metadata: notebook2Metadata as NotebookEntryMetadata,
    },
  },
  {
    slug: "local-llm-journey",
    sourcePath: "content/notebook/local-llm-journey/index.mdx",
    source: "export const metadata = {\n  id: \"local-llm-journey\",\n  type: \"notebook\",\n  title: \"Why local models keep pulling me in\",\n  description:\n    \"A reflection on local LLMs, unfinished thoughts, and the pull toward more private, cost-aware AI workflows.\",\n  publishedAt: \"2026-07-19\",\n  updatedAt: \"2026-07-19\",\n  status: \"published\",\n  tags: [\"LLM\", \"local-ai\", \"privacy\", \"workflow\"],\n  series: {\n    slug: \"building-the-workshop\",\n    title: \"Building the workshop\",\n    order: 3,\n  },\n  related: {\n    notebook: [],\n    lab: [],\n  },\n  featured: false,\n};\n\n## The cloud-shaped default\n\nAI-assisted development gave me the spark to tinker more privately again.\n\nThat sounds slightly backwards at first, because most AI-assisted development starts somewhere else. The useful tools are usually cloud tools. The model runs on someone else's machine, the prompt travels over the network, and the default setup is not private in the way a local notebook or local script is private. The tools are convenient, fast, and often better than anything I can run on my own machine. I do not want to pretend otherwise.\n\nBut the more I use them, the more I notice a small friction point that does not go away: I do not always want to send the thing I am thinking about to a monthly service.\n\nSometimes the data is not secret in a dramatic way. It is just unfinished. A rough idea, a private note, a work-in-progress prompt, a small part of a project that I do not want to hand over yet. I also want to collect those fragments so I can use them later, no matter which LLM provider I end up using. That makes local models attractive for a second reason: the notes and context can become mine first, instead of being shaped around one service from the start. The problem is not only whether a provider stores it or trains on it. The problem is that the work leaves my machine at all.\n\nThat is the part that keeps making local models interesting to me.\n\n## The local pull\n\nI am not deep into self-hosting yet. This is still something I am feeling my way into. The appeal is simple though: if the model runs locally, the boundary is clearer. The prompt, the context, and the messy half-formed thought can stay where I am already working.\n\nThere is a cost side to this too. Since I started using LLMs, I have been aware of how easily the bill can become invisible. A model feels cheap when you use it once. It feels different when it becomes part of how you write, code, search, summarize, and debug. I have tried to build workflows that do not treat every task as if it deserves the most expensive model available.\n\nThat habit is not specific to local models. It applies to cloud models as well. Some tasks need strong reasoning. Some need speed. Some only need a cheap pass over text. I already think in those categories when I choose tools, even if the choice is still manual most of the time.\n\nLocal models make that question sharper because the resources are more visible. There is only so much memory, disk space, and patience. A general model can be useful, but specialized models are part of what makes local use realistic. A smaller model that is good enough for one task may be better than a larger model that tries to do everything.\n\n## The model-choice problem\n\nThat creates the next problem.\n\nIf different tasks work better with different models, I do not want to be the router every time. I do not want to stop and think: is this prompt for coding, writing, summarizing, searching, planning, or something else? Which model is installed? Which one is already loaded? Which one is worth the wait?\n\nTools that route between models already exist, and I am aware that this is not a new idea. What I am trying to understand is more personal and practical: what would a good version look like for my own workflow?\n\n## The question I keep coming back to\n\nThe open question for me is not how to build the smartest possible routing system. It is smaller than that, and probably more useful: how far can I get with a simple, cost-aware way to decide which model should handle a prompt? Locally, that decision has another cost attached to it. If I can only keep one model running at a time, then changing the task may also mean unloading one model and loading another. The choice is not only about quality. It is also about waiting, memory, and whether the switch is worth it.\n\nMaybe the answer is a small classifier. Maybe it is a set of rules. Maybe it is another model that only decides where the prompt should go. I do not know yet. That is why this belongs in the workshop.\n\nFor now, I mostly want to put the question in the room. Privacy, sovereignty, and cost are enough to make local models worth my attention, but the practical shape is still unclear.\n\nIf the answer turns into something I can tinker with, it will probably become a Lab entry. Until then, the question is enough to follow: how efficient can this get if the default assumption is not \"send it away,\" but \"give unfinished thoughts a local place to grow?\"\n",
    module: {
      default: notebook3Component,
      metadata: notebook3Metadata as NotebookEntryMetadata,
    },
  },
  {
    slug: "the-notes-i-never-take",
    sourcePath: "content/notebook/the-notes-i-never-take/index.mdx",
    source: "export const metadata = {\n  id: \"the-notes-i-never-take\",\n  type: \"notebook\",\n  title: \"The notes I never take\",\n  description: \"Lowering the friction between initial thoughts and useful notes.\",\n  publishedAt: \"2026-07-29\",\n  updatedAt: \"2026-07-29\",\n  status: \"published\",\n  tags: [\"local-ai\", \"notes\", \"workflow\", \"curiosity\"],\n  series: {\n    slug: \"building-the-workshop\",\n    title: \"Building the workshop\",\n    order: 4,\n  },\n  related: {\n    notebook: [],\n    lab: [\"sparktrail-mvp-plan\"],\n  },\n  featured: false,\n};\n\n## The moment before the note\n\nMost of my ideas disappear before they become anything.\n\nThe reason is usually boring: writing it down takes just enough effort that I do not do it. I notice the spark, think I should capture it, and then I start thinking about the work around the note. Open the right place. Explain the thought clearly enough. Give it a title. Clean it up just enough so I can understand it later.\n\nBy then, the moment has already become heavier than the idea.\n\nThe Workbench is supposed to be built around curiosity, so this is not a small problem for me. I want the site to hold more than finished things. The lab can show experiments and rough edges. The notebook can keep the slower part: why I started, what I was trying to understand, and what changed while I followed the question.\n\nBut for that to work, the first capture step has to be almost frictionless.\n\n## Messy capture is not enough\n\nOne answer would be to lower my standards and write messier notes.\n\nIn theory, I could just throw every idea into a file, an app, or a scratchpad and sort it out later. That would at least preserve the thought. It would be better than losing it completely.\n\nIn practice, that kind of capture often has the same result. A note can be too messy to use, too disconnected to find, or too vague to understand later. It still exists somewhere, but it has no clear path back into the work.\n\nThat is the friction I keep noticing. A dumping ground is easy to create. The harder thing is a place where unfinished thoughts land in a form I can actually work with.\n\nI do not want polished writing at the moment of capture. I want enough structure that future me has a chance.\n\n## Why voice keeps coming back\n\nVoice feels like the right first step because it is faster than typing out a proper note.\n\nWhen an idea is still forming, speaking is easier than writing. I can circle around the thought, repeat myself, correct myself, and leave gaps without stopping the flow. That fits the way curiosity usually starts for me. I do not know where the spark will lead me yet. Often I do not even know what the actual point is.\n\nRaw voice recordings do not solve much on their own. They are easy to create and easy to ignore. A folder full of audio files can become just as useless as a folder full of messy notes.\n\nEven structured notes can get lost later. I want to solve that too, eventually. For now, I am looking at the first step: lowering the friction enough that the thought gets captured before it disappears.\n\nTranscription helps, but only halfway. A transcript turns the recording into text, which makes it searchable and easier to scan. Spoken text is usually not the kind of text I want to find later, though. It has filler words, repeated starts, unfinished sentences, and ideas arriving in the wrong order. The transcript preserves the moment. It does not automatically preserve what made the moment useful.\n\n## Two passes instead of one\n\nThe version I want to build needs two passes.\n\nThe first pass would turn audio into text. There are already models that are specialized for that, and this part of the problem feels specific enough that it should not be handled by a general chat model pretending to do everything.\n\nThe second pass would take the transcript and turn it into something more coherent. I do not mean finished prose. I also do not want the model to rewrite so much that the original thought disappears. I want a structured note: filler removed, repetitions folded together, fragments grouped in a sensible order, and the actual idea easier to pick up again.\n\nI know this is not a completely new concept. [Ghost Pepper](https://matthartman.github.io/ghost-pepper/) is close to the shape I have in mind: record something, transcribe it, then use another model to make sense of the result. It is tied to macOS, though, and I do not want this workflow to depend on always being on one platform.\n\nGetting there is part of the fun. Building this is also a reason to learn: which models are good enough, where the integration gets awkward, and what kind of software makes this feel like a real part of my workflow instead of another tool I have to remember to use.\n\n## Keeping the first thought local\n\nLocal AI is part of the appeal here for the same reason it keeps pulling me in elsewhere.\n\n<ContentLinkCard type=\"notebook\" slug=\"local-llm-journey\" label=\"Related notebook entry\" />\n\nThese early thoughts are not always private in a dramatic way. They are not necessarily secrets. They are unfinished, and unfinished thoughts feel different from finished text. I do not always want the first rough version of an idea to leave my machine before I have even decided whether it matters.\n\nRunning the transcription and cleanup locally would make the boundary clearer. The audio, the transcript, and the structured note could stay close to where the idea happened. That does not solve everything, but it changes the default. The thought does not have to be sent away before it has a place to grow.\n\nCost matters too. If this becomes a habit, I do not want every small spoken thought to turn into another cloud-model call by default. Local processing makes the cost easier to understand and contain. Time may matter less than it does in an interactive chat, because the processing does not have to happen while I stare at the screen. If I can trigger it and let it run in the background, slower local models are easier to accept.\n\n## The first layer of the system\n\nI have wanted the Workbench to become a system for lowering the friction around curiosity from the start. The site is already split between notebook and lab because I want to keep both sides of learning: the thinking and the making. A voice-to-note workflow is only the first layer.\n\nA structured note is useful on its own, but it can still disappear into the background. It can sit in the wrong folder, stay disconnected from related ideas, or become another thing I technically saved but never return to. Eventually I want these notes to feed into a local second brain: a system that can add a note to an existing topic when it belongs there, or create a new one when it does not.\n\nI could try to solve that brain layer first. It would probably help me sort the ideas I already have and make it easier to start working on them. But my motivation right now is with the transcription tool. That is where the spark is, and I want to follow it while it is still bright. A lost idea feels worse than spending longer structuring ideas that already exist somewhere.\n\nThat is why I want to start here. I do not need another note-taking app, and transcription itself is not new. I want a better path between the moment curiosity appears and the point where it can become part of the work.\n\nI am looking forward to seeing where that path leads.\n",
    module: {
      default: notebook4Component,
      metadata: notebook4Metadata as NotebookEntryMetadata,
    },
  },
  {
    slug: "welcome",
    sourcePath: "content/notebook/welcome/index.mdx",
    source: "export const metadata = {\n  id: \"opening-the-workshop\",\n  type: \"notebook\",\n  title: \"Opening the workshop\",\n  description:\n    \"Why I am starting this site as a place for writing, experiments, and learning in public.\",\n  publishedAt: \"2026-07-18\",\n  updatedAt: \"2026-07-18\",\n  status: \"published\",\n  tags: [\"meta\", \"nextjs\", \"mdx\", \"introduction\"],\n  series: {\n    slug: \"building-the-workshop\",\n    title: \"Building the workshop\",\n    order: 1,\n  },\n  related: {\n    notebook: [],\n    lab: [],\n  },\n  featured: false,\n};\n\nI wanted a place that feels closer to a workshop than a portfolio.\n\nA portfolio usually asks: what have you finished? A workshop asks a more interesting question: what are you exploring, what are you learning, and why?\n\nThat is the energy I want this site to have. I want a place for ideas that are more useful than another saved bookmark, but not so polished that every note has to pretend to be final.\n\nAI-assisted development made that feel more possible again. It is easier now to move from “I should look into that someday” to a small working experiment while the curiosity is still fresh. That changes the weight of building. The code still matters, but it is not the whole story.\n\nThe part I want to preserve is not only what I built. It is why I built it, what I was trying to understand, what surprised me, and what I learned from the process. That is why this site has two connected spaces.\n\n<Callout title=\"Notebook and Lab\" tone=\"note\">\n  The Notebook is for the thinking behind the work. The Lab is for the technical shape of the work.\n</Callout>\n\nThe Notebook is where I slow things down. If I keep coming back to a question, a tool, or a pattern, writing gives me a way to make it clearer. It is the place for motivation, tradeoffs, lessons, and the parts that code does not explain.\n\nThe Lab is the more concrete side. Some things are easier to understand when they run somewhere real. A small demo can reveal constraints that a paragraph would hide. Sometimes the useful part is not the finished result, but the edge where something almost works and the next question becomes obvious.\n\nThat is also how I want to write about this site itself. This note is about the reason for the workshop. The implementation belongs in the Lab: how the site uses Next.js App Router, generic slug routes, repo-owned MDX, and TSX demos to keep the technical side visible.\n\n<ContentLinkCard type=\"lab\" slug=\"content-architecture\" label=\"Related Lab entry\" />\n\nThis is only the starting point. The site, the split between Notebook and Lab, and the way I write about experiments will change as I use it. That is part of the point: I want the workshop to evolve with what I learn instead of pretending the structure was obvious from day one.\n\nThe goal is to keep following curiosity, and to leave enough of a trail that it might help someone else (or future me) follow theirs too.\n",
    module: {
      default: notebook5Component,
      metadata: notebook5Metadata as NotebookEntryMetadata,
    },
  }
];

